{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>Upon a stranger</b> — who is not needed for priesthood or kingship. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 30:33). And Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] answered that זָר in this place means anyone who is not of the seed of Aaron and his sons who are mentioned (Exodus 30:30), as in \"and the stranger who comes near shall be put to death\" (Numbers 1:51) — any stranger apart from those mentioned; and likewise \"and any stranger shall not eat holy things\" (Leviticus 22:10), \"so that no stranger who is not of the seed of Aaron may approach\" (Numbers 17:5). Therefore he would maintain that the anointing of Solomon (I Kings 1:39), where Zadok brought the horn of oil out from the Tent — and it was the anointing oil — was a provisional ruling by decree of prophecy. But this is not the view of our Sages (Keritot 6a).\n\nAnd I say: Scripture states (Exodus 30:31), <b>This shall be to Me a holy anointing oil throughout your generations</b>. Now it would have been fitting for it to say, \"This shall be a holy anointing oil to Aaron and to his sons throughout their generations,\" just as it said concerning the garments (Exodus 29:29), \"And the holy garments that are Aaron's shall be for his sons after him\"; or for it to say, \"And Aaron and his sons you shall anoint and consecrate them to minister to Me, and it shall be for them an everlasting statute throughout their generations\" — for since now it mentioned only Aaron and his sons themselves, and said that it shall not be given upon a stranger, the implication would be upon every man except those mentioned themselves; and so too it says (Exodus 30:32), \"Upon the flesh of a man it shall not be poured,\" and Scripture elsewhere specifies (Leviticus 6:15), \"the priest who is anointed in his stead from among his sons.\"\n\nRather, this is the matter: He commanded that Aaron and his sons be anointed with it now, and said, <b>This shall be to Me a holy anointing oil</b> — to anoint with it the anointed of My holiness whom I shall choose, <b>throughout your generations</b>, and it shall not be given upon a stranger who is not Mine. Therefore kings and High Priests are anointed with it, for both of them are anointed of HaShem, as it is written (Psalms 89:21), \"I have found David My servant, with My holy oil have I anointed him\" — with oil that is a holy anointing unto Me.\n\nAnd the meaning of <b>Upon the flesh of a man it shall not be poured</b> is a warning concerning every man, for it did not say \"upon the flesh of a stranger it shall not be poured.\" The reason is: one may pour from the oil upon the head of Aaron to anoint him, and anoint his sons as well, but one must not smear with it the flesh of any man whatsoever, even the anointed priests — in the manner that one rubs flesh with fine oils, and they smear the flesh with hands anointed with them after bathing, as in \"and wash yourself and anoint yourself\" (Ruth 3:3), \"and I washed you with water and anointed you with oil\" (Ezekiel 16:9). And this is the plain sense and meaning of the verse.\n\nAnd thus our Sages said (Keritot 7b): From where do we derive that a High Priest who took of the anointing oil that is upon his head and put it upon his intestines is liable? For it is stated: \"Upon the flesh of a man it shall not be poured.\""], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:19:21.384629484Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}